<figure class="case-lead-image case-lead-burgundy"><img src="/img/portfolio/digital-access-advisory-presentation-mockup.webp" width="2400" height="1600" alt="Digital access advisory presentation shown as a complete system of risk, lifecycle and decision tools."></figure>

<section class="case-summary"><div><p class="eyebrow">The problem</p><h2>The audience needed to understand access risks well enough to decide when and where to act.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>This self-initiated example explores communication for technical advisory work. A list of access risks can create awareness, but it does not show relative priority or where intervention belongs in a programme.</p><p>The presentation therefore moves through three jobs: establish why design-stage decisions matter, prioritise specific risks and locate advisory input across the lifecycle.</p></div></section>

<section class="decision-band"><span>03</span><div><p class="eyebrow">The decision</p><h2>Turn a broad concern into a sequence of practical choices.</h2></div></section>

<section class="section-shell service-offer">
  <p class="eyebrow">The tool sequence</p>
  <div class="service-offer-grid">
    <article><span>01</span><h3>Reframe the issue</h3><p>Connect early design assumptions with the access consequences they create later.</p></article>
    <article><span>02</span><h3>Define the risk</h3><p>Establish a shared language before asking people to compare priorities.</p></article>
    <article><span>03</span><h3>Prioritise action</h3><p>Use severity and likelihood to separate urgent risks from those to monitor.</p></article>
    <article><span>04</span><h3>Show where to intervene</h3><p>Locate specialist input across the programme lifecycle and make early engagement explicit.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-outcome"><div><p class="eyebrow">The practical output</p><h2>An editable presentation and a set of tools for review and discussion.</h2></div><p>This is self-initiated work, created to demonstrate how our <a href="/services/information-design/">information design</a> can communicate technical advisory ideas. The risk matrix can support a workshop; the lifecycle diagram can help commissioners decide when to engage expertise.</p></section>

